The hero alternates between generated photographs of a steel bridge and an industrial pipe rack. These illustrate connected infrastructure; they are not photographs of Aberration hardware or customer installations. The signal charts illustrate a stream leaving its calibrated reference band; they are not live telemetry or measured results.

## From raw signal. To meaningful deviation.

A continuous path from connected telemetry to evidence you can act on. Built around the relationships in your data.

1. **Connect — read the system.** Bring your data streams together. Observe measurements in the context of the system they belong to.
2. **Calibrate — define normal.** Learn from baseline data. Calibrate anomaly scores with conformal methods to establish a meaningful reference.
3. **Detect — find the break.** Accumulate evidence as observations arrive. Surface changes that depart from the calibrated baseline.
4. **Understand — follow the evidence.** Review detected changes alongside the telemetry behind them. Move from an anomaly to a clearer picture.

## A threshold tells you when. Context tells you why.

An isolated reading is only part of the story. Follow how signals relate, how evidence accumulates, and where a system starts to change.

- **Temporal:** Follow change as observations arrive.
- **Multivariate:** Read relationships across data streams.
- **Calibrated:** Ground anomaly scores in baseline data.
- **Reviewable:** Keep the signal beside the evidence.

## Statistical rigor. Operational reality.

Detection is more than an unusual data point. Aberration brings streaming pipelines, conformal calibration, and accumulated evidence into one system. So you can investigate change with a meaningful reference.

